WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.236 --> 00:00:04.685
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.314 --> 00:00:10.494
Mexicans are murderers and rapists.

00:00:13.315 --> 00:00:16.780
Black kids are super predators.

00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:22.510
Gun owners are monsters and witches.

00:00:26.571 --> 00:00:27.210
Jews are rats.

00:00:31.752 --> 00:00:33.880
Dehumanization is nothing new.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.919
It's been around forever and
it's come from all sides.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:44.720
And yet it remains the single greatest
threat to America's social fabric.

00:00:49.127 --> 00:00:55.366
Now I know some of you may be thinking,
why is Noah the right messenger for this?

00:00:55.366 --> 00:00:56.479
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:00:56.479 --> 00:00:59.204
I've heard it all over the last few weeks.

00:00:59.204 --> 00:01:03.147
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Noah, you're white.

00:01:03.147 --> 00:01:05.885
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Noah, you're a man.

00:01:05.885 --> 00:01:09.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Noah, you're straight.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:10.942
Just go back to the golf course.

00:01:10.942 --> 00:01:15.520
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.790
&gt;&gt; But that's actually

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:17.909
exactly why I'm here.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.460
Because I believe that
beginning to deescalate

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.460
the risk from dehumanization starts with
a little bit of personal responsibility.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:31.132
So I'd like to share
an idea with you today, and

00:01:31.132 --> 00:01:33.190
it's an idea that some of
you might disagree with.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.890
But I hope you know it comes from a place
of wanting to preserve this incredible 250

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:44.130
year old experiment with democracy
that is the United States of America.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:46.770
It goes like this,

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.040
perhaps that in beginning to repair
our social fabric from the bottom up.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.650
Perhaps instead of leaning into all
of the things that make us different,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.910
all the things that draw us apart.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.670
Instead of trying to take back all of
the negative stereotypes that exists about

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:10.110
each and every one of our identify groups,
just maybe it's time to re embrace

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:15.480
that old idea of the great American
melting pot not to destroy identity

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:21.040
but to build a shared identity that
is greater than the sum of its parts.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.750
And I believe that the way to do that is
to engage with people on their values and

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:34.580
ideas on their merits Now, why do I
think this is an idea we're spreading?

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.000
Why is this a problem that
urgently needs addressing?

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.160
I actually don't think you have
to look too far to see it.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.930
In our last election we had one
candidate demonize an entire country,

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:51.369
we had another candidate call half of
the voters of this country deplorable.

00:02:52.888 --> 00:02:58.153
Tragedies like 9/11, brought
an incredible generation of Americans,

00:02:58.153 --> 00:03:03.418
who gave up established careers to stand
shoulder to shoulder in fighting for

00:03:03.418 --> 00:03:05.200
our freedom and our flag.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.240
Today, tragedies seem to
produce arguments about who or

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.029
what we should ban from this country.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:17.720
But it's not just politics, the private
sector plays a role in this, too.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:22.670
Think about the different ways that you're
asked to define yourself, more narrowly.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:28.520
On Facebook you have to summarize your
activities and interests on a page.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.270
LinkedIn, all of your work and
accomplishments on a profile.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:41.615
One picture, a one line biography to help
people decide if they want to date you.

00:03:41.615 --> 00:03:45.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The media is complicit in this too.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.620
It's gotten to the point where all
that's covered seems to be complete and

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.300
utter outrage or
strict adherence to political correctness.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.230
And political correctness
comes from both sides.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:04:00.340
The right is just as politically
correct about things like standing for

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:05.390
the national anthem as the left is about
things like abortion or immigration.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:08.960
The result is the creation of almost
a pseudo religion around certain ideas or

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.680
groups so that we can't even have serious
conversations about the social issues that

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.409
matter most to this country We

00:04:19.409 --> 00:04:24.330
know how this ends,
we've seen it time and time again.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:29.030
Create the other, ostracize it,
vilify it and destroy it.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:33.170
It's happening in Myanmar today,
it happened in Rwanda in 1990s and

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.919
yes it happened in Europe in the 40s.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:41.150
We can not stand to see America's
social fabric unravelled.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.560
Now I'm not talking about just top down
institution building, I'm talking about

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.250
the things that we can each do to stitch
it back together from the bottom up.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:54.120
And that comes back to these idea of
engaging people on their values and

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.080
ideas on their merits.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.020
So what has that meant for me?

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:02.000
Well, it's meant that I've tried
to immerse myself in ideas and

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.940
places that I wouldn't usually go,
adding places on the left like

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.742
The Huffington Post, Buzzfeed,
places on the right like Breitbart,

00:05:10.742 --> 00:05:14.830
Drudge Report,
even as far right as The Daily Stormer.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:16.813
From these experiences, and

00:05:16.813 --> 00:05:20.143
from conversations with
people in this room, and

00:05:20.143 --> 00:05:26.540
those far beyond I believe that there is
a way to stitch this fabric back together.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:30.920
So let's start by talking about what it
means to engage people on their values.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.120
Some of you may know of a senator
from Ohio by the name of Rob Portman.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:40.200
Senator Portman is a Republican,
he was historically quite conservative.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.770
And notoriously against gay marriage.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.540
He was being vetted as Mitt Romney's
potential running mate in 2012.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:50.260
Around that time, Senator Portman's
son came out to him as gay.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:55.200
Now, like any good senator, he waited for
a politically astute moment.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.940
But eventually,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.090
&gt;&gt; Eventually he came out and

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.280
spoke very powerfully on this issue.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.130
He changed his position and
said I am doing so not as a politician,

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.930
not as a conservative, but
as a dad who loves his son a lot.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:16.140
And wants him to have the same
opportunities that his sister might have.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:21.681
The values of equality of opportunity, the
values of a dad who loves his son a lot,

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:26.200
a parent who loves their son a lot,
should resonate with all of us.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:33.900
If I'm to criticize credibly I have to
acknowledge appropriately when I see

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:38.360
the values, that I care for
being embodied by our leaders.

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.160
Which brings me to the second
part of this solution.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.280
What does it mean to engage
ideas on their merits?

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.380
And to that I'm going to offer
you all three challenges today.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.679
The first.

00:06:50.679 --> 00:06:54.428
Put down the pitchforks.

00:06:54.428 --> 00:06:58.802
Step back from the politics of blame.

00:06:58.802 --> 00:07:03.594
This is much easier said than done,

00:07:03.594 --> 00:07:08.238
I fall victim to this all the time.

00:07:08.238 --> 00:07:13.260
It is so easy to vilify,
it is so easy to express anger.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:18.480
But the reality is, it doesn't work.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:22.252
It pushes people's backs against the wall,

00:07:22.252 --> 00:07:26.700
it makes them feel like they're defending
closely held identities and beliefs.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:31.109
It actually produces
the cycle of radicalization.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.580
Which leads me to my second challenge.

00:07:37.952 --> 00:07:41.120
Forgo labels.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:46.310
See if you can go a week without
using words that end in ist or

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:48.390
ism, those ist and isms.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:49.540
They're easy to throw out.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.150
How many times you heard the conversation,
that's racist.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:55.690
No no no, I'm not racist!

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:56.630
Or, that's sexist.

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:58.410
No no no, I'm not sexist!

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:03.200
It's not that racist or sexist ideas
need to go without being called out,

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:08.600
but naming is far less
powerful than explaining, and

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:14.360
produces that same dynamic
of radicalization.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:19.269
Which brings me to my third and
final challenge, don't be

00:08:19.269 --> 00:08:23.470
afraid of ideas.

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.420
If I can accept when I'm in a classroom or

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:30.790
I'm at work that I am learning when
I am challenged, when I am pushed

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:35.740
beyond my comfort zone Why do I not feel
the same way about my political ideology.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:40.785
What does this look like?

00:08:40.785 --> 00:08:45.560
Well I'm hoping you all might
model that with me today.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:51.683
Some of the ideas I have shared
came from things I've read deep on

00:08:51.683 --> 00:08:54.838
the far left from
the radical Saul Alinsky or

00:08:54.838 --> 00:09:00.054
all the way here on the right from
provocateurs like Milo Yiannopoulos.

00:09:00.054 --> 00:09:03.089
If I told you all that when you walked
into the room some of you might've

00:09:03.089 --> 00:09:04.050
walked out.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:09.700
But the idea is that the people
are far less important than the ideas.

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:14.940
So, if you agreed with
what I had to say or

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.140
you liked it, great, let's talk about it.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:23.260
If you hated it or you're angry at me
right now, great, let's talk about it.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:25.639
But either way we gotta talk about it.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:31.650
Because I believe that the single most
important thing that this generation can

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:36.426
do is keep this great multicultural

00:09:36.426 --> 00:09:41.512
democratic experiment going.

00:09:41.512 --> 00:09:43.142
Thank you.

00:09:43.142 --> 00:09:45.152
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:45.152 --> 00:09:47.409
[MUSIC]

